@*
    The board's status column, as one component.

    The direction contract for this app says "the queue is the interface" and fixes the
    vocabulary at four values — QUEUED, WORKING, DONE, FAILED — but the vocabulary was
    only ever a CSS convention. Bulk Generate spelled it out in a four-case switch;
    Studio wrote its own bare strings with no lamp; the progress
    bar had no status word at all. Three surfaces, three renderings, and nothing stopping
    a fourth from inventing a fifth value — which is the exact drift the contract forbids.

    So it is a type now. `BoardStatus` is the only set of states a row may be in, and
    this component is the only thing that draws them. A caller supplies its own wording
    (`Ready`, `Working`, `Cutting the track…`) but cannot supply its own state.
*@
<span class="status status--@Status.ToString().ToLowerInvariant() @Class" role="@(Status == BoardStatus.Failed ? "alert" : null)">
    @if (Status == BoardStatus.Working)
    {
        @* Only a working row is lit. A lamp on a queued row would make "waiting" and
           "running" look the same from across the concourse, which is the one thing a
           departure board must never do. *@
        <span class="lamp lamp--lit" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    }
    else if (ShowLamp)
    {
        <span class="lamp" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    }
    @(Label ?? DefaultLabel)
</span>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public BoardStatus Status { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Wording for this row. Falls back to the status' own name.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Label { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Draw an unlit lamp on non-working rows, so the column aligns down the board.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ShowLamp { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }

    private string DefaultLabel => Status switch
    {
        BoardStatus.Working => "Working",
        BoardStatus.Done => "Done",
        BoardStatus.Failed => "Failed",
        _ => "Queued",
    };
}
